{"id": "9e9a6efd-101f-489c-a27e-8f07b12db8df", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "With one account to analyze, I'll split the work into two parallel sub-agent tasks: one evaluating IAM principal permissions via policy simulation, and another inventorying access keys, their last-used timestamps, and CloudTrail Bedrock invocation activity.

Found one connected account, 123456789012. I'll now check which IAM users have Bedrock model access (including via groups/policies) and review access key age, last use, and actual Bedrock activity in parallel.

"}]}

{"id": "c58d52b5-6f5d-4c6e-b314-1c6fb2d23481", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"id": "tooluse_3kKD3kd6ipHoQzXSE9DN4L", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-iam-permissions", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Audit which IAM users in AWS account 123456789012 have effective permission to invoke Amazon Bedrock models. READ-ONLY; do not modify anything.",
  "context": "Security audit for a report. We need the full list of IAM users that can call bedrock:InvokeModel, InvokeModelWithResponseStream, Converse, ConverseStream (and optionally bedrock:* / agent/knowledge-base invoke actions such as bedrock:InvokeAgent, bedrock:Retrieve*), directly or via group membership, inline/managed policies, or permissions boundaries. Also note SCPs/boundaries that would block it if visible.",
  "resources": ["AWS_ACCOUNT:123456789012"],
  "timeframe": "current state (now = 2026-11-20T09:24Z)",
  "criteria": "For each user: name, ARN, how access is granted (policy name + whether via user, group, or managed policy such as AdministratorAccess, PowerUserAccess, AmazonBedrockFullAccess, AmazonBedrockReadOnly (does NOT allow invoke)), whether wildcard resources are allowed (all models vs specific model ARNs), any Deny/conditions/permissions boundary, and whether the user has console login profile and MFA. Prefer iam:simulate_principal_policy with ActionNames bedrock:InvokeModel, bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream, bedrock:Converse, bedrock:ConverseStream against resource arn:aws:bedrock:*::foundation-model/* to get effective results, and fall back to reading policies. Also list roles briefly only as a count/short note (focus is users). Return a concise table plus counts. Use region us-east-1 for IAM calls."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Audit IAM users' effective Bedrock invoke permissions"}, {"id": "tooluse_GdUcAudgaf2K7MKc4z9seM", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-keys-activity", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["investigating-infrastructure-changes"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "In AWS account 123456789012, inventory all IAM users' long-lived access keys (and Bedrock API keys / service-specific credentials for Bedrock if listable, e.g. iam:ListServiceSpecificCredentials) and determine which keys have actually been used for Amazon Bedrock. READ-ONLY; do not modify anything.",
  "context": "Security audit report on who can/does invoke Bedrock models with long-lived credentials. Another analysis covers effective IAM permissions; you cover the credential hygiene and observed usage side.",
  "resources": ["AWS_ACCOUNT:123456789012"],
  "timeframe": "past 90 days up to 2026-11-20T09:24Z",
  "criteria": "For every IAM user access key: user, key ID (access key ID is fine, never secrets), status (Active/Inactive), creation date and age in days, last used date, last used service and region (iam:GetAccessKeyLastUsed), whether user has >1 key. Flag keys older than 90 days, unused >90 days but still active, and keys whose last-used service is bedrock. Then use CloudTrail (lookup_cloudtrail_events; look for eventSource bedrock.amazonaws.com, InvokeModel/Converse/etc., and note events also may be in other regions such as us-west-2, eu-central-1 etc. — check us-east-1 and any region where Bedrock activity appears) to identify which IAM users/access key IDs actually invoked Bedrock models, counts, models, source IPs, and any anomalies (unusual IPs, user agents, regions, high volume, ListFoundationModels/ GetFoundationModelAvailability probing, access denied bursts). Also check Bedrock model invocation logging config and whether any Bedrock API keys exist. Return concise tables with counts."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Inventory access keys and Bedrock usage via CloudTrail"}], "created_at": null}

